$DIR/main.p:21:1: cannot pass &g to (*Guard).Drop (called when g is dropped), which keeps it after returning
    &g points into n. n is a local variable of Scope (declared at main.p:18).
    (*Guard).Drop (called when g is dropped) stores a pointer loaded from g.hits
    in package variable last (main.p:12), so &g would outlive n.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If (*Guard).Drop (called when g is dropped) should keep its own copy of n,
    change it to take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an
    owned[int] from new, a shared[int], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:26:22: cannot pass &newGuard(&n) to (*Guard).Drop (called when newGuard(&n) is dropped), which keeps it after returning
    &newGuard(&n) points into n. n is a local variable of Temporary (declared at
    main.p:25). (*Guard).Drop (called when newGuard(&n) is dropped) stores a
    pointer loaded from g.hits in package variable last (main.p:12), so
    &newGuard(&n) would outlive n.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If (*Guard).Drop (called when newGuard(&n) is dropped) should keep its own
    copy of n, change it to take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can
    keep: an owned[int] from new, a shared[int], or a library owner such as a
    vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
